# Week 1 Notes — First model formulation

## Objective

The goal this week was to translate a biological hypothesis from regenerative muscle biology into a computational abstraction that could be simulated.

The biological question:

Can a small number of corrected nuclei rescue neighbouring nuclei through spatial signal propagation inside a multinucleated muscle fibre?

Rather than attempting to reproduce experimental data, the aim was to construct a conceptual simulation framework capable of exploring whether amplification through spatial spread is plausible.

---

## Biological interpretation

Muscle fibres contain many nuclei distributed along a shared cytoplasm. Experimental evidence suggests corrected nuclei can influence surrounding regions, possibly through diffusion of corrective molecular products.

This motivates modelling the fibre as a spatially structured system rather than independent compartments.

---

## First abstraction

The fibre was represented as:

a 1-dimensional chain of nuclei

Each nucleus:

- produces signal if corrected
- receives signal from neighbours
- becomes rescued if signal exceeds threshold

This produces a minimal interacting system with local coupling.

---

## First update rule

Signal propagation was initially implemented using neighbour averaging:

u_i(t+1) = average(neighbour signals)

This was chosen because:

- it approximates local diffusion behaviour
- it is simple to implement
- it provides immediate intuition about spatial smoothing

However, this rule has no explicit physical interpretation yet.

---

## Known weaknesses

The neighbour averaging rule:

- is heuristic
- lacks parameter interpretation
- cannot be extended easily to irregular geometries
- does not explicitly include decay

This motivated transitioning toward a Laplacian formulation in Week 2.

---

## Implementation decisions

The simulation was written using pure Python lists rather than NumPy arrays to make each update step transparent.

This helps verify correctness before introducing vectorisation.

---

## Key insight this week

Even simple neighbour coupling produces rescue amplification beyond the corrected nuclei themselves.

This suggests spatial transport alone may explain part of the observed biological amplification effect.